The user just asked to “move the logo up and to the right.” In the HTML/CSS for the site, the logo positioning is controlled by the `.hero-logo` rule. I previously tried to edit that rule, but the edit failed because the file had been changed since I last read it, so the old_string I supplied no longer matched the current file content. 

I need to re‑read the relevant section to get the exact current text so I can supply a matching old_string. Using the `Read` tool I fetched lines 158‑166, which show the `.hero-logo` block exactly as it appears now:

```
.hero-logo {
    position: absolute;
    right: 2rem;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
}
```

The user wants the logo shifted rightward (i.e., farther to the left edge) and upward. To move it right we set `right: 0;` (instead of `2rem`). To move it up we lower the `top` percentage from `50%` to something like `32%`, which will raise the logo relative to its container. The `transform: translateY(-50%)` is already centering the logo vertically based on the `top` value, so I’ll leave that unchanged.

Now I need to construct the edit operation. The `old_string` must match the exact snippet I just read, including line breaks and indentation. The `new_string` will be the same block but with the two property values changed as described. I’ll use the `Edit` tool with `replace_all: false` because I only want to replace this single occurrence.

Thus the correct edit call is:

```json
{
  "replace_all": false,
  "file_path": "/home/lane/MythosMini/slm_alliance.html",
